// whole-list — THE FOLDED LIST RESIDUAL, which no other row reaches. Added
// 2026-08-27 while building F29.
//
// The other list rows leave a residual with a known PREFIX — `[Order o, ..]`,
// `[n]` — and a list pattern constrains a prefix, so those spell themselves with
// the grammar F22 already had. This one leaves the whole list type. `list<Order>`
// is `[] | [Order, ..]`: two spines, and `l_str/1` folds them back into
// `list<Order>` so that an ordinary list type does not read like a residual.
//
// THAT FOLD IS WHY F29 §1 PROPOSED A GRAMMAR CHANGE, and why it is not needed.
// Inheriting the fold on the head channel collapses two spines that each have a
// pattern into one that has none, which made `pattern -> lident '<' type_list
// '>' lident` look like the only way to spell the row. F29.2 — in the same file
// — had already made a residual N HEADS, and once it is N heads the answer is
// the two clauses an author writes:
//
//     Ship([]) -> ...
//     Ship([Order o, ..]) -> ...
//
// So this fixture's job is to keep the head channel from re-inheriting the fold.
// If it ever prints one head again, that is the regression.
module WholeList

record Order { Id: int, Total: int }

type Job = list<Order> | :none

public atom Ship(Job j)
Ship(:none) -> :nothing
